## 1. Hardware & wiring

| Part | Connection |
|---|---|
| ESP32 DevKit V1 | any classic WROOM board |
| ADS1115 ADC | SDA -> GPIO **21**, SCL -> GPIO **22**, VDD 3.3 V, GND common (addr 0x48) |
| Analog pH module | out -> ADS1115 **A0**, VCC per module, GND common |
| Analog TDS module | "A" out -> ADS1115 **A1** |
| Analog turbidity module | out -> **its existing two-resistor voltage divider** -> ADS1115 **A2** |
| DS18B20 temperature | DATA -> GPIO **4** + **4.7 k pull-up** to 3.3 V |

No sensor connects to the ESP32's own ADC pins (GPIO 32/34/35 are unused).

**Input voltage safety (ADS1115 powered at 3.3 V):**
- Absolute maximum input is **VDD + 0.3 V ≈ 3.6 V** — and the gain setting
  does NOT make a higher voltage safe; it only changes the measurement range.
- Your turbidity module was measured at **~3.5–3.72 V** at its output. It must
  reach A2 only through the divider. **Before first power-up, measure the
  divider output with a multimeter** with the sensor in air: it must read
  **≤ 3.3 V** (ideally ≤ 3.0 V).
- If the divider output can exceed 3.3 V, do not power the board: increase the
  divider ratio (e.g. two equal resistors 10k/10k halve the voltage →
  ~1.75–1.86 V) and re-measure. Do not raise the sensor supply voltage.
- pH (typically 0–3.3 V) and TDS (typically 0–2.4 V) outputs fit the  ±4.096 V range — still verify each with a multimeter once before trusting.
- Keep the existing common-ground arrangement.
```

