## 1. Executive summary

HydraSense is a four-layer system: ESP32 firmware (C++/Arduino) → MQTT (Mosquitto) → FastAPI + SQLAlchemy/PostgreSQL backend → React 19/Vite/TypeScript frontend, with a scikit-learn/XGBoost ML side-project under `ml/`.

The backend is the strongest part: telemetry validation, NULL-tolerant storage, per-row turbidity units, provenance tags, a central four-parameter suitability engine with a closed input contract, database-backed rate limiting, RBAC and an honest ML path are all **Implemented** and covered by 408 passing tests.

The weak spots are concentrated at the two ends of the pipeline:

- **Firmware calibration is inconsistent with your own measurements** — the pH constant in the sketch (1.0278 V @ pH 7, 59.16 mV/pH) reproduces neither your pH 7 nor pH 4 buffer readings, and the `config.h` calibration flags the documentation describes are **dead code** (never read by the sketch).
- **Several frontend screens still render mock data** (Command Center KPIs, Station Detail, Water Sources, public check-water/nearby/warnings, parts of Live Monitoring and Analytics), and one public page still advertises a **dissolved-oxygen** value the hardware cannot measure.

Secrets are handled correctly by policy (`JWT_SECRET_KEY` has no code default, `.env` and `config.h` are gitignored — verified), but three concrete security gaps remain: the local JWT secret is only **9 characters**, MQTT is **anonymous/no-TLS on the LAN**, and rate limiting covers only login/register.

