## 5. pH calibration implementation

**Formula actually in code** ([hydrasense_esp32.ino:56–58, 165–190](firmware/hydrasense_esp32/hydrasense_esp32.ino#L165)):

```c
const float PH_VOLTAGE_AT_PH7 = 1.0278f;
const float PH_VOLTS_PER_PH_UNIT = 0.05916f;   // theoretical Nernst slope at 25 °C
// ...
const float estimate = 7.0f - ((r.voltage - PH_VOLTAGE_AT_PH7) / PH_VOLTS_PER_PH_UNIT);
if (!isfinite(estimate) || estimate < 0.0f || estimate > 14.0f) {
    r.error = "pH estimate outside 0..14; check PH_VOLTAGE_AT_PH7 against a known pH 7 buffer";
    return r;   // publishes null
}
```

| Question | Answer | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Calibration constants | 1.0278 V at pH 7; slope fixed at theoretical 59.16 mV/pH (single point) | **Implemented** |
| Units/source of voltages | Volts at the **ADS1115 A0 input**, gain ±4.096 V → 125 µV/LSB (`countsToVolts`/`lsbVoltsForGain`) — **not** the ESP32's own ADC | **Implemented** |
| Two-point calibration | `CAL_PH_V7` / `CAL_PH_V4` exist in [config.h](firmware/hydrasense_esp32/config.h) with placeholder 2.50/3.00 and `PH_CALIBRATED 0` — **the sketch never references any of them** | **Not implemented** (dead config) |
| Consistency with your measurements | **No** — see below | **Partially implemented** |

### Comparison with your ADS1115 divider-junction measurements

Feeding your values through the firmware's formula (computed this pass, nothing written):

| Your measurement | Voltage | Firmware would report | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| pH 7 buffer (initial) | 1.33 V | pH **1.89** | wrong |
| pH 7 buffer (subsequent) | 1.238–1.254 V (mid 1.246) | pH **3.31** | wrong |
| pH 4 buffer (initial) | 1.54 V | pH **−1.66** | rejected → publishes `null` |
| pH 4 buffer (subsequent) | 1.472–1.496 V (mid 1.484) | pH **−0.71** | rejected → publishes `null` |

Additional discrepancies:

1. **Reference point is off by ~0.22 V.** Your later pH 7 readings centre on **1.246 V**, not 1.0278 V. With the current constant, a real pH 7 buffer reads ≈3.3 and a real pH 4 buffer falls below 0 → firmware nulls the value.
2. **Slope does not match either.** Your pairs imply **≈79.3 mV/pH** (later set) and **≈70.0 mV/pH** (initial set), against the hard-coded theoretical 59.16 mV/pH. Nernst theory at 25 °C also predicts pH 4 at `1.246 + 3×0.05916 = 1.423 V`, but you measured ≈1.484 V — so a two-point fit of *your* data would give a steeper slope than the code assumes. (Your measurements are, as you noted, preliminary and unverified; I have not substituted anything for them.)
3. **Comment/code mismatch in the sketch.** The block comment says "*1.1378 V shifts that same sample to ~pH 7*" while the constant is **1.0278**. One of the two is stale.
4. **`config.h` calibration flags are dead.** `PH_CALIBRATED`, `CAL_PH_V7`, `CAL_PH_V4`, `TURBIDITY_CALIBRATED`, `CAL_TURB_*`, `TDS_CALIBRATED` appear only in [config.h](firmware/hydrasense_esp32/config.h)/[config.example.h](firmware/hydrasense_esp32/config.example.h). [firmware/README.md §10 and the troubleshooting row "Analog values NULL despite ADS ready — calibration flags at 0"](firmware/README.md) describe behaviour the sketch does not have: **values are published regardless of the flags**, and `AnalogResult.calibrated` is hard-wired `false`.
5. **Possible undocumented hardware divider on the pH path.** The wiring table documents the pH module going **directly** to A0 (only turbidity has a documented divider). Your phrase "ADS1115 divider junction" suggests a divider may exist on the pH line too; if so, the firmware's volts-per-pH constant would need the divider ratio folded in. **Unable to verify** from source alone.

