Both "specifications" come from the same standard — **BIS IS 10500:2012 (Amd. 4)** — applied by two rule engines in the backend, depending on which screen you're on.

## 1. Four-sensor screening engine (the main one)

Used by **Check My Water** (personal) and **Live Monitoring → live assessment**. Defined in [water_screening.py](backend/app/services/water_screening.py) as `ScreeningCriteria`, wrapped by the enforced façade [suitability_engine.py](backend/app/services/suitability_engine.py). Criteria version `live-screening-2026.10.3`.

### Drinking water

| Parameter | Acceptable | Permissible | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| pH | **6.5 – 8.5** (no relaxation) | — | BIS IS 10500 Table 1 |
| TDS | **≤ 500 mg/L** | **≤ 2000 mg/L** (absence of alternate source) | BIS IS 10500 Table 1 |
| TDS (project target) | **< 200 mg/L** | — | project demo preference, not BIS |
| Turbidity | **1 NTU** | **5 NTU** | BIS IS 10500 Table 1 — *only when a calibrated NTU value exists* |
| Temperature | no pass/fail limit | — | "context and trend only" |

Plus eight **required-but-unmeasured** laboratory parameters that always block a positive conclusion: E. coli (absent/100 mL), nitrate 45 mg/L, fluoride 1.0/1.5, arsenic 0.01/0.05, lead 0.01, hardness 200/600, chloride 250/1000, sulfate 200/400 mg/L.

### Domestic use (non-drinking)

Identical numeric criteria — **pH 6.5–8.5, TDS 500/2000 mg/L, turbidity 1/5 NTU** — with two deliberate differences:

- The **strict <200 mg/L target is never applied** (it is drinking-only; controlled by `DEMO_STRICT_TDS_RULE`, default on).
- Only **three** required lab parameters instead of eight: E. coli, hardness, chloride.

And the standing caveat: *"Domestic water that can be ingested must satisfy the full drinking-water requirements."*

### What changes the verdict

| Trigger | Result |
|---|---|
| pH outside 6.5–8.5, or TDS > 2000, or NTU turbidity > 5 | `NOT_RECOMMENDED_BY_SCREENING` (outside) |
| TDS 500–2000, or turbidity 1–5 NTU, or TDS < 200 (drinking only) | `REVIEW_RECOMMENDED` (caution) |
| Everything in range | `WITHIN_PROJECT_TARGETS` (within) |
| Missing/invalid reading, stale > **300 s**, unstable sensor, or live reading with unverified calibration | `INCONCLUSIVE` (insufficient) |

Any single implausible value (pH outside 0–14, TDS > 5000 mg/L, temperature outside −5…60 °C, index outside 0–100) collapses the whole assessment to `INCONclusive` — it never rides along with a pass.

**Turbidity caveat:** our sensor reports a **relative index 0–100**, so it is never compared with the 1/5 NTU limits. The status becomes `not_comparable` with the explicit text *"Turbidity cannot be compared with NTU limits… a laboratory test with a calibrated NTU instrument is required."* Since turbidity is rated for drinking/domestic/general, an unvalidated turbidity reading **caps the best possible result at caution** — "pH and TDS pass" is never rendered as "all requirements passed." Alert bands are separate project bands: index **≥ 55** warning, **≥ 85** critical ([alert_thresholds.py](backend/app/services/alert_thresholds.py)).

## 2. Standards-based suitability engine

Used by the **public QR station page** and the **authority station suitability endpoint** ([public.py](backend/app/api/v1/public.py), [authority.py](backend/app/api/v1/authority.py)). Defined in [water_suitability.py](backend/app/services/water_suitability.py).

- `drinking` → BIS IS 10500: pH 6.5–8.5, TDS 500/2000, turbidity 1/5 NTU, plus 8 required lab parameters.
- `domestic` → same three criteria, plus 3 required lab parameters (E. coli, hardness, chloride).

Results are `preliminary_suitable / treatment_required / outside_limits / lab_confirmation_required / insufficient_data`, and because the required lab parameters are never available from a four-sensor station, **a "suitable" verdict is structurally unreachable** — you get `lab_confirmation_required` at best. Both carry the limitation *"passing pH/TDS/turbidity never certifies drinking-water safety."*

**Why two engines:** the public/authority screen cites the standard and its unmeasured parameters explicitly (so the citizen sees what's missing), while the personal/live screen gives an operational four-sensor state (within / review / not recommended / inconclusive) for a quick read. Both are pure rules — no ML input, no LLM in the verdict path.

